  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is heat radiation?",
      "a": "Radiation is heat carried by invisible infrared rays, a kind of light. It travels in straight lines, needs no air, and is how a fire warms your face from a distance.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a fire warm you from far away?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is convection?",
      "a": "Convection is heat carried by a moving liquid or gas. Hot air or water expands, becomes lighter and rises, while cooler fluid sinks to take its place.",
      "asks": [
        "How does convection work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is conduction?",
      "a": "Conduction is heat passed through a material by touching particles. Hot, fast-jiggling particles bump their neighbours and pass the energy along, which is why metal handles heat up.",
      "asks": [
        "How does conduction work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is it warmer above a fire than beside it?",
      "a": "Above the fire you get radiation plus the rising stream of hot air and gases from convection. To the side you mostly get radiation alone.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is smoke above a fire so hot?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Which way of moving heat works through empty space?",
      "a": "Only radiation. Conduction and convection need particles to pass heat along, but infrared rays can cross empty space, which is how the Sun warms the Earth.",
      "asks": [
        "Can heat travel through a vacuum?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Radiation is the only one of the three ways that can carry heat across empty space.",
    "Infrared radiation travels at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres per second."
  ],
